Writer/Instructor and Script Consultant Shannan E.&nbsp;
Johnson (The Professional Pen): Let me be specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:06.880
about those beats because I’ve said that word a&nbsp;
lot but I’m talking specifically about the normal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.400
the inciting incident, the goal or the problem.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.440
Film Courage: What is your process&nbsp;
for teaching screenplay structure?

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:26.960
Shannan: I start with the Pixar formula. I go&nbsp;
into character work. I am someone in my own time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:33.120
who likes to psychoanalyze things. I'm always&nbsp;
asking people Why? How? What happened to you?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.880
So we do some work like that for the characters.&nbsp;
The more that you understand people, the more you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.840
can understand your characters who are people. In&nbsp;
my class which may make some people uncomfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.320
but in my class we talk about ourselves&nbsp;
a lot. We're doing a lot of inner work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.960
because my class is also about fighting writer's&nbsp;
block and in my opinion writer's block comes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.520
lack of confidence and lack of preparation so&nbsp;
we spend a lot of time talking about confidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.120
talking about what our fears are, what our&nbsp;
flaws are, what our wants are, what's standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.600
in the way of those wants. Why? Because that's&nbsp;
what's happening for your characters. What do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.280
they want? What do they need? What's standing&nbsp;
in the way of that. What are afraid of? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.000
are their flaws that are going to get in the&nbsp;
way of them getting the thing that they want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.080
so if you start to understand how your own life&nbsp;
the life of your tribe members I call my classes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:26.640
tribes the life of your tribe members if you start&nbsp;
to understand why they do the things they do then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.200
you start to understand characters a little bit&nbsp;
more and now your characters can be more authentic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:38.560
in their journey because oftentimes I can feel&nbsp;
the writer making characters do things versus it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:44.240
feeling like an actual decision that the character&nbsp;
would make so we spend some time on characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.240
so i'd say that's week two that's actually just&nbsp;
what I did on a couple of days ago week two that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:55.360
was last night it was just last night it's hard&nbsp;
to keep up with time these days it's all one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:03.920
it's all one big thing then we start to talk about&nbsp;
goal setting because every single story is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.120
your protagonist solving a problem reaching a goal&nbsp;
that's why we are here so we have to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.600
what's the best goal for them to reach or who's&nbsp;
the best character for that goal depending upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.240
which one you begin with and then we start&nbsp;
talking about now how are they physically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:24.240
going to achieve it because again going back to&nbsp;
they need to save the world well that's general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.920
what are they going to do how are they going to&nbsp;
save it and start asking ourselves well what are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.440
the things that get in the way so in the fourth&nbsp;
week we start talking about the complications we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:43.600
started talking about the stakes in week three&nbsp;
we also talk about world building because every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:51.280
single story you have to teach us the rules of&nbsp;
that world if it's just regular old present-day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.240
earth something that we see in the first three&nbsp;
minutes are gonna tell me that that's what it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:03.280
so you're still teaching me if it's a new industry&nbsp;
like I love to use The Devil Wears Prada I'm not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.840
in the fashion industry so you have to teach me&nbsp;
how it works here so they open up by showing me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:14.160
how all the other women are nicely dressed and our&nbsp;
protagonist is not they show us by when she gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.680
into that office how everyone is looking at her&nbsp;
judging her and then when Meryl Streep's character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.920
is going to show up everybody it's chaotic because&nbsp;
they're trying to make sure they get in line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.520
I'm learning about this industry I'm learning&nbsp;
about what the rules are here even if there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.080
not much being said out loud so we talk about&nbsp;
that and especially if you're doing something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.360
that's science fiction fantasy supernatural horror&nbsp;
then there are rules you have to set up I need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:45.040
know candyman comes when I say it how many times&nbsp;
because if not then I don't have the suspense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.720
now if I see a person standing in the mirror and&nbsp;
they only say it twice should I be afraid because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.400
I don't know how many times they're supposed&nbsp;
to say it if I understand they have to say it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.000
five times I don't even remember if it's five or&nbsp;
three but they have to say it x amount of times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.360
and now I know when she gets on number four that I&nbsp;
should be on the edge of my seat but I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:09.600
that if you haven't given me the information so&nbsp;
we talk about world building and then in the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.360
week that's when we finally start talking about&nbsp;
outlining because I want people to know there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:18.720
not one way to do it you can do it a million&nbsp;
different ways so I show them different examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:25.760
of how you can outline but all of those things&nbsp;
that we're learning basically break into those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.560
large beats and let me be specific about those&nbsp;
beats because i've said them said that word a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:37.440
but I'm talking specifically about the normal&nbsp;
the inciting incident the goal or the problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:46.160
getting into act two knowing what your midpoint is&nbsp;
knowing what your rock bottom is knowing what your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:51.520
point of no return is which some people kind&nbsp;
of mix into that rock bottom midpoint place it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.400
basically just means we get to a place where&nbsp;
something changes so much that our character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.760
has to change tactics that's just all it means&nbsp;
everybody gives it a different word but then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:05.520
we got to get up to our climax which is your&nbsp;
highest point when your bad guy is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.600
meet your good guy or your character is going&nbsp;
to meet their problem head on and then we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:15.600
to come down to the resolution where we say what&nbsp;
have they won because they succeeded or what did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:21.280
they lose because they didn't yet throughout that&nbsp;
entire journey we were following them emotionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:28.000
so even if they didn't succeed we still feel&nbsp;
satisfaction with the story so all of those beats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:34.880
are what I mean when I'm saying structure all&nbsp;
of those beats fit into your act one act two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:39.760
and act three and so I utilize those&nbsp;
different weeks to talk about those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:46.960
different elements because they all break&nbsp;
into those big beats and how can you tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:50.400
and I realize this is very specific and&nbsp;
we don't have something to workshop but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:58.480
just vaguely when someone is really forcing an&nbsp;
action from their character it can be because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:04.080
evidence is just not on the page or the evidence&nbsp;
is on the page and your character isn't doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:10.720
what you've told us that they do so if you've said&nbsp;
this is a really shy character every single thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:16.000
you've shown us is that they're shy and then on&nbsp;
page 15 they stand on top of a table and dance

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:25.200
what that's not who you told me this person was so&nbsp;
unless you showed me something that motivated this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:32.400
then now I know you just needed her on a table&nbsp;
